2026-10-27T11:52:26.9375005Z [0m[1m[38;5;9merror[unsound][0m[1m: `AsyncFd::new` and related constructors violate I/O safety[0m
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9375876Z     [0m[36m┌─[0m /github/workspace/Cargo.lock:128:1
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9376482Z     [0m[36m│[0m
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9377432Z [0m[36m128[0m [0m[36m│[0m [0m[31mtokio 1.49.0 registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index[0m
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9379343Z     [0m[36m│[0m [0m[31m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[0m [0m[31munsound advisory detected[0m
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9381284Z     [0m[36m│[0m
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9381838Z     [0m[36m├[0m ID: RUSTSEC-2026-0386
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9382454Z     [0m[36m├[0m Advisory: https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0386
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9385575Z     [0m[36m├[0m `tokio::io::unix::AsyncFd` provides safe constructors (`new`, `with_interest`, `try_new`, and `try_with_interest`) that accept any `T: AsRawFd` rather than requiring proof of ownership or validity of the file descriptor. Because `RawFd` implements `AsRawFd`, safe code can construct an `AsyncFd` from an arbitrary or closed file descriptor, or close/replace the underlying file descriptor while the `AsyncFd` is still alive.
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9387993Z       
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9388232Z       This violates I/O safety in two ways:
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9388575Z       
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9389596Z       1. `AsyncFd<T>` implements `AsFd` for all `T: AsRawFd` by calling `BorrowedFd::borrow_raw(self.as_raw_fd())`, allowing safe code to obtain an arbitrary `BorrowedFd<'_>` without owning the file descriptor.
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9392297Z       2. Tokio's I/O driver stores a pointer to an internal allocation in the `epoll`/`kqueue` user data for registered file descriptors. If the underlying file descriptor is duplicated and then closed while an `AsyncFd` is alive, and the OS reuses that file descriptor number for a another IO resource on the same runtime, dropping the first `AsyncFd` deregisters the reused file descriptor number and frees the first while the original file description remains registered in `epoll`, leading to a use-after-free when a readiness event fires.
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9394389Z       
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9395905Z       Starting in Tokio 1.51.6, 1.53.3, and 1.54.0, `AsyncFd::new`, `AsyncFd::with_interest`, `AsyncFd::try_new`, and `AsyncFd::try_with_interest` are deprecated in favor of the `unsafe` constructors `AsyncFd::register` and `AsyncFd::register_with_interest`. Callers must ensure that the file descriptor remains valid, open, and unchanged for the lifetime of the `AsyncFd`.
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9397536Z     [0m[36m├[0m Announcement: https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/pull/8587
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9398214Z     [0m[36m├[0m Solution: Upgrade to >=1.51.6, <1.52.0 OR >=1.53.3 (try `cargo update -p tokio`)
