2026-10-27T11:52:26.9432468Z     [0m[36m│[0m [0m[31m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[0m [0m[31msecurity vulnerability detected[0m
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9433391Z     [0m[36m│[0m
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9433673Z     [0m[36m├[0m ID: RUSTSEC-2026-0398
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9434170Z     [0m[36m├[0m Advisory: https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0398
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9434810Z     [0m[36m├[0m `ZSTD_seekable_decompress` shortens the requested length to the end of the
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9435414Z       data without checking that the offset is before it. For an offset past the
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9435938Z       end, it returns a wrapped-around length without writing anything, and
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9436437Z       `Seekable::decompress` passed that length to `Vec::set_len`.
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9436816Z       
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9437297Z       With the `seekable` feature, whoever supplies the archive, or chooses the
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9437821Z       offset, can leave the caller's `Vec<u8>` with a length far beyond its
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9438355Z       capacity. Reading it then discloses neighbouring heap memory, and appending
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9438908Z       to it corrupts memory. Offsets near `u64::MAX` can also hang the call.
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9439301Z       
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9439629Z       The flaw was corrected by clamping requests to the end of the data, and by
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9440181Z       checking every count reported by the C library against the buffer capacity.
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9440982Z     [0m[36m├[0m Announcement: https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/80506be743fae2ed0c2fe8b21c374c6a2eab0be7
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9441719Z     [0m[36m├[0m Solution: Upgrade to >=8.2.0 (try `cargo update -p zstd-safe`)
2026-10-27T11:52:26.9442204Z     [0m[36m├[0m zstd-safe v7.2.4
